<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n12872" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dēlēgātĭo</span></b> ,
			ōnis, f.
		delego, <i>no.</i> II., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>an assignment, substitution,
				delegation</i> of one person by another. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b> Prop.: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">a mancipe,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Att. 12, 3, 2</span></i>
			; 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">of one thing for another: delegatione perfecta
					solutio,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Ben. 6, 5, 2</span></i>
			; cf. context.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">necesse est ipse laborem impendas: delegationem
					res ista non recipit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Ep. 27, 4</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
